RPCS3 with a native RTX Remix render backend
============================================

This build submits PlayStation 3 geometry to the RTX Remix runtime through the
Remix API, so a PS3 title is path traced rather than rasterised. It is a research
build, not a product. Read the "What works" section before filing anything.

Source and full documentation:
  https://github.com/BRAGme/rpcs3-rtx


TWO THINGS ARE NOT IN THIS ZIP AND IT WILL NOT RUN WITHOUT THEM
---------------------------------------------------------------

1. THE REMIX PLUS RUNTIME.

   This backend does NOT work with the stock NVIDIA RTX Remix runtime. Stock is
   on the 0.6.x API line; this build needs the Remix Plus fork, which reserves
   REMIXAPI_VERSION_MINOR = 1000. The version handshake rejects stock outright.

   Get it here:
     https://github.com/RemixProjGroup/dxvk-remix   (maintainer: Kim2091)
     Release tag: remix-plus-1.5.1
     Asset:       Remix_Plus_v1.5.1_x64_games_release.zip

   That release is the one to use, and it is what this build is tested against.
   The vendored API header is byte-identical to 1.5.1's copy, so the handshake
   and every interface slot match exactly.

   OPTIONAL, for SHARC. The backend is developed against a newer, untagged build
   of the same fork -- Kim2091/dxvk-remix `revised-9-10`, commit 38082acf --
   which adds SHARC, a spatially hashed world-space radiance cache, as
   rtx.integrateIndirectMode = 3. There is no release tag for it; it exists only
   as a CI artifact, reproducible with

     gh workflow run build.yml --repo BRAGme/dxvk-remix --ref <branch at 38082acf>

   The API is unchanged between the two, so this is purely opt-in. On that build
   the f90 / specular-level options are spelled rtx.legacyMaterial.* rather than
   rtx.opaqueMaterial.*. Your rtx.conf comes with the runtime, not with this
   zip; writing both spellings into it is harmless and is correct on either
   build. If the runtime beside rpcs3.exe is not the exact binary this build was
   baselined on, the log says so at startup and carries on -- that line is a
   note, not an error.

   Unzip it into a subfolder called  remix\  next to rpcs3.exe:

     rpcs3.exe
     remix\d3d9.dll
     remix\...

   DO NOT put d3d9.dll directly beside rpcs3.exe. Anything else in the process
   that resolves that name would pick it up. The subfolder is mandatory, not a
   convention. If you keep the runtime elsewhere, set RPCS3_REMIX_DLL to the
   full path of its d3d9.dll instead.

   It is not bundled here because that folder contains third-party redistributables
   (nvngx_dlss*.dll, cudart64_13.dll, GFSDK_Aftermath, libxess.dll,
   amd_fidelityfx_vk.dll) with their own terms, inside someone else's fork build.
   Get it from the people who made it.

2. PS3 FIRMWARE. Same as stock RPCS3 -- install it yourself from a PS3UPDAT.PUP
   you own. No firmware, games or keys are included or ever will be.


ENABLING THE BACKEND
--------------------

  Settings -> GPU -> Renderer -> RTX Remix

There is also a Settings -> RTX Remix tab for the backend's own knobs. Every knob
also has an RPCS3_REMIX_* environment variable, which always wins over the GUI.
The full table is in docs/remix/KNOBS.md in the repository.

PER-GAME REMIX PROFILES, new in preview 2. A file named <TITLEID>.conf next to
rpcs3.exe is read once at backend init and pushed into the environment before any
knob is read. BLUS30094.conf ships here: 217 measured settings for Haze that until
now existed only in a launcher script on the developer's machine and reached nobody
who downloaded a build. If you played preview 1 and wondered why it looked worse
than the screenshots, this is why.

A variable already set in your environment always beats the file, so a launcher
script still overrides it and an A/B harness stays authoritative.

The load is reported in remix_dump.log as a line beginning "Remix game-config:",
naming the file, how many keys it applied, how many it skipped because they were
already set, and how many it ignored. If a setting seems to do nothing, read that
line first -- and note the config is read ONCE, so changing it needs a restart.

Writing one for another title is the single most useful thing you can contribute.
See CONTRIBUTING.md in the repository; you do not need git, and the one rule is to
label which numbers you measured and which you guessed.

RPCS3's own per-title configs in config\custom_configs\ are separate and still ship
for a few titles, applying
automatically when you run those titles.


WHAT WORKS, AND WHAT DOES NOT
-----------------------------

Nine titles have had real work done on them. They are not equally exercised, so
what each one establishes is listed rather than a "works / doesn't" verdict.
Eight of them ship a tuned <TITLEID>.conf beside rpcs3.exe -- those files are
commented, and reading one is the fastest way to see what tuning a title means.

  Resistance 2 (NPEA00431)  -- the primary development title, and where nearly
    every measurement comes from: camera recovery from vertex-program ucode,
    world transforms, texcoords and albedo, both skinning families, per-draw
    blend state, sky classification, 2D UI compositing.

  Haze (BLUS30094)          -- a second, structurally different engine. It skins
    on the SPU, so vertices reach RSX already animated, and it has no 'layered'
    vertex programs at all. Matching ADD's src2 constant here recovered 71% of
    world transforms that had been drawing at identity. This title is the
    evidence the ucode approach is not fitted to one game.

  Minecraft: PS3 Edition (NPUB31419) -- camera parity, quantised chunk meshes,
    and the texture-cache design. Texture identity is descriptor-only rather
    than content-sampled because of a measurement here: content-rehashing
    dragged mesh creation from 5,881 creates / 887 live to 188,427 / 31,793
    over 9,120 frames, because the albedo hash folds into the mesh key.

  Ratchet & Clank Collection (BCUS98282) -- renders as of preview 3, once three
    stacked faults were cleared: the window hand-off when the collection menu
    exitspawns into a game, restarting the runtime afterwards, and a projection
    matrix this title writes across several places instead of one. Its sky
    renders but is unlit, its HUD needs Composite render target draws turned
    on, and the framerate is low.

  The Last of Us (BCUS98174) -- new in preview 4. Reaches the menu and Hometown
    gameplay on update 1.11. Cutout coverage -- foliage, hair, the Hometown
    window masks -- is taken from the texture channel the shader actually tests
    rather than from the colour map's alpha channel, which is opaque in this
    title and used to turn every card and pane solid.

  Saints Row 2 (BLUS30201)  -- character, material and skinning work. It is
    carried by four unit-test suites that run in every build, rather than by a
    captured frame.

  Eat Lead (BLUS30267)      -- the largest profile here at 57 settings. This
    title paints its flat "digital" effects from a colour held in a
    fragment-program constant rather than in a vertex attribute, so the
    vertex-colour route cannot reach them; RPCS3_REMIX_FPCONSTALBEDO states
    that literal instead. The knob is 0 globally, but BLUS30267.conf sets it
    to 7 -- all three routes -- so it is already on for this title. The blood
    pixels and the text render correctly under it, confirmed by play-test. The
    title's other "digital" effects -- objects disappearing, the destroyed-
    object warp -- still do not come through, and this route does not explain
    them.

  Demon's Souls (BLUS30443) -- the game's authored light tables, extracted and
    compiled into the build.

  GRAW 2 (NPUB30502)        -- boots through its child-process hand-off. The
    runtime is shut down without being unloaded, because the Reflex PCL-stats
    ping thread outlives Shutdown and unloading it crashes.

Not working / known bad:
  - A subset of draws still lands at runaway positions and reads as white shards.
  - Frame rate swings hard with view complexity: ~55 fps in a bounded interior,
    ~12 fps on a wide outdoor draw list.
  - Haze's shadow-receiving draws land untextured. They bind a 2048x2048 DEPTH16
    shadow map on the lowest referenced texture unit and the ucode names no
    albedo unit, so 55,360 draws in one capture reached Remix with no texture.
    The workaround that walks past that refusal is in this build and is
    build-verified, not run-verified. RPCS3_REMIX_RETRYUNSUP=0 reverts it.
  - Anything not named above is untested, which is not the same as broken.
  - Keyboard input is dead under Remix unless rtx.useNewGuiInputMethod = False
    is set in rtx.conf. Controllers are unaffected.

The repository README is honest about the rest and cites the measurements.


BUILD PROVENANCE
----------------

  rpcs3.exe built from  BRAGme/rpcs3-rtx  branch remix-backend, commit @GIT_SHORT@,
  reporting @GIT_REV@.
  Everything here is buildable from that commit; see BUILDING.md in the repo,
  and the "Known traps on Windows" section of the README, which will save you
  the four failures we hit.

RPCS3 is GPL-2.0. This is a modified fork; the complete corresponding source is
the repository linked at the top.
